-The depth variance loss $L_{DV}$ is computed by comparing the predicted depth variance against the threshold $\tau$. While the exact functional form (e.g., Huber, L2) is not explicitly detailed in the provided text, the constraint is defined by the threshold $\tau = 1.5\delta_0$ [S1]. The loss penalizes variances exceeding this threshold to enforce compactness.
+The depth variance loss $L_{DV}$ constrains the computed depth variance against the threshold $\tau$ [S1]. The exact functional form of the penalty (e.g., L1, L2, or Huber) is not specified in the sources [S1]. The variance for a ray $r$ is computed as $\hat{D}_{var}(r) = \sum_{i} T_i (1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \delta_i)) (\hat{D}(r) - t_i)^2$, where $\hat{D}(r) = \sum_{i} T_i (1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \delta_i)) t_i$ is the expected depth [S2, G]. The loss applies $\lambda_{DV}$ to the variance when it exceeds $\tau$ [S1].
 
